1
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:04,490
A heart is placed on a scale.

2
00:00:04,710 --> 00:00:09,531
On the other side sits a symbol
of what is right and true.

3
00:00:09,751 --> 00:00:16,035
Standing beside the scale is a god
with the head of a canine: Anubis.

4
00:00:16,255 --> 00:00:20,331
He is famous as the fearsome
judge of the dead.

5
00:00:20,551 --> 00:00:23,936
But look closely at the ancient sources,

6
00:00:23,936 --> 00:00:28,166
and you will find that Anubis
does not judge alone.

7
00:00:29,067 --> 00:00:31,617
So what was Anubis actually doing?

8
00:00:31,837 --> 00:00:35,743
Watching over tombs, protecting
the bodies of the dead, guiding

9
00:00:35,743 --> 00:00:39,963
them on their journey, and checking
the scale that weighs the heart.

10
00:00:40,183 --> 00:00:46,585
In this episode, we follow sculptures and
papyri at The Metropolitan Museum of Art

11
00:00:46,585 --> 00:00:50,932
to see, one by one, the work
Anubis does in the sources.

12
00:00:51,833 --> 00:00:55,697
On screen you will see photographs
of real museum objects,

13
00:00:55,697 --> 00:00:59,968
along with reconstructions created
to help you picture the past.

14
00:01:00,188 --> 00:01:03,591
Every reconstruction
is labeled in the top corner.

15
00:01:03,811 --> 00:01:06,147
Let's begin with the first object.

16
00:01:08,267 --> 00:01:11,381
First, this statue of a reclining canine.

17
00:01:11,601 --> 00:01:15,514
Its front legs stretch forward
and its ears stand upright.

18
00:01:15,734 --> 00:01:19,125
It seems less asleep than
alert, keeping watch.

19
00:01:19,345 --> 00:01:22,610
This statue at the Met
is carved from limestone

20
00:01:22,610 --> 00:01:24,953
and was originally painted black.

21
00:01:25,173 --> 00:01:28,269
The god was not always
shown in human form.

22
00:01:30,067 --> 00:01:32,791
The museum's description draws attention

23
00:01:32,791 --> 00:01:35,651
to the link between
canines and cemeteries.

24
00:01:35,871 --> 00:01:39,709
Animals that roamed burial
grounds could disturb the dead.

25
00:01:39,929 --> 00:01:45,164
Yet a watchful canine could also be
imagined as a power that guarded them.

26
00:01:45,384 --> 00:01:48,944
This is an interpretation
of the image's background,

27
00:01:48,944 --> 00:01:51,737
not proof of a single origin for Anubis.

28
00:01:54,300 --> 00:01:59,527
In another statuette, an animal
head sits on a human body.

29
00:01:59,747 --> 00:02:05,471
It is made of wood, and fine details
of the clothing still survive.

30
00:02:05,691 --> 00:02:09,293
The museum describes
it as probably Anubis,

31
00:02:09,293 --> 00:02:13,924
because more than one god
was shown with a canine head.

32
00:02:14,144 --> 00:02:18,889
A familiar look does not always
tell us the name for certain.

33
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:22,126
This is where dates matter.

34
00:02:22,346 --> 00:02:26,391
The statues we have just
seen and the royal relief

35
00:02:26,391 --> 00:02:30,271
we will see later were not
made in the same era.

36
00:02:30,491 --> 00:02:34,905
Ancient Egypt covers
an enormous span of time.

37
00:02:35,125 --> 00:02:40,074
To understand a god's form
and role, we need to ask not

38
00:02:40,074 --> 00:02:44,747
only what is depicted, but
when the object was made.

39
00:02:46,567 --> 00:02:51,609
One of Anubis's most important roles
was his connection to mummification.

40
00:02:51,829 --> 00:02:55,283
In ancient belief, preparing
the body of the deceased

41
00:02:55,283 --> 00:02:59,599
and performing rituals helped
sustain their existence after death.

42
00:02:59,819 --> 00:03:03,090
Preserving the body was not
only about appearance.

43
00:03:03,310 --> 00:03:05,818
It was tied to the hope of living again.

44
00:03:07,667 --> 00:03:13,253
A gilded coffin made for a priest named
Nedjemankh was once displayed at the Met.

45
00:03:13,473 --> 00:03:17,036
The museum's description
points to a scene of a mummy

46
00:03:17,036 --> 00:03:20,394
on a bier, with Anubis
performing rites beside it.

47
00:03:20,614 --> 00:03:25,008
Isis and Nephthys also appear
as protectors of the mummy.

48
00:03:25,228 --> 00:03:28,980
The coffin was later
found to have been looted,

49
00:03:28,980 --> 00:03:31,997
and in 2019 it was returned to Egypt.

50
00:03:32,900 --> 00:03:35,800
Of course, it was living
people who prepared

51
00:03:35,800 --> 00:03:38,295
the dead and readied them for burial.

52
00:03:38,515 --> 00:03:41,843
The stories of the gods
gave meaning to that work.

53
00:03:42,063 --> 00:03:47,851
Human hands and divine protection:
together, they shaped the funerary rites.

54
00:03:52,367 --> 00:03:56,718
The idea of protection appears
beyond statues of the god, too.

55
00:03:56,938 --> 00:04:00,733
The ritual object called
an imiut combines a staff,

56
00:04:00,733 --> 00:04:03,997
a jar, and a form wrapped
in an animal skin.

57
00:04:04,217 --> 00:04:08,139
According to the museum,
objects like this and guardian

58
00:04:08,139 --> 00:04:12,352
figures were connected to sacred
spaces and funerary rites.

59
00:04:12,572 --> 00:04:15,005
Protecting the dead took many forms.

60
00:04:16,833 --> 00:04:23,529
Now we move from preparing the body
to the story of the journey after death.

61
00:04:23,749 --> 00:04:26,389
Here we meet the Book of the Dead.

62
00:04:26,609 --> 00:04:33,156
The museum notes that its ancient name
was the Book of Going Forth by Day.

63
00:04:33,376 --> 00:04:37,968
It is a collection of spells
and images on papyrus,

64
00:04:37,968 --> 00:04:41,641
meant to help the deceased on their way.

65
00:04:42,567 --> 00:04:45,790
On screen now is a papyrus
that accompanied

66
00:04:45,790 --> 00:04:48,400
the burial of a woman named Nauny.

67
00:04:48,620 --> 00:04:52,245
The museum dates it to around 1050 BCE.

68
00:04:52,465 --> 00:04:56,137
The scroll holds images
and texts for several spells,

69
00:04:56,137 --> 00:05:00,091
and among them is the scene
of the weighing of the heart.

70
00:05:00,311 --> 00:05:05,889
It was made not for an abstract dead
person, but for one particular individual.

71
00:05:06,800 --> 00:05:08,302
Why the heart?

72
00:05:08,522 --> 00:05:13,460
Writing about another funerary
papyrus, the museum explains

73
00:05:13,460 --> 00:05:18,993
that the heart was considered
the center of intellect and emotion.

74
00:05:19,213 --> 00:05:24,525
In the weighing scene, how a person
had lived is what is being tested.

75
00:05:24,745 --> 00:05:30,299
The heart was shown not as a mere
organ, but as a record of a life.

76
00:05:31,200 --> 00:05:35,914
On the other side of the scale
is something representing Maat.

77
00:05:36,134 --> 00:05:39,780
Maat is linked to order
and to what is right.

78
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:44,965
Many people picture only
a feather, but in Nauny's papyrus,

79
00:05:44,965 --> 00:05:49,160
Maat appears as a small
goddess wearing a feather.

80
00:05:49,380 --> 00:05:55,691
It is worth remembering that one
famous image does not fit every source.

81
00:05:56,600 --> 00:06:00,227
Here is another scene
of Anubis and the scale.

82
00:06:00,447 --> 00:06:06,524
But this image is not a photograph
of the ancient wall painting itself.

83
00:06:06,744 --> 00:06:09,361
It is a copy on paper, made

84
00:06:09,361 --> 00:06:14,209
by Nina de Garis Davies
from the tomb of Nakhtamun.

85
00:06:14,429 --> 00:06:18,526
It is a valuable way
to study the imagery, but we

86
00:06:18,526 --> 00:06:23,049
present the original
and the copy as different things.

87
00:06:24,867 --> 00:06:28,067
Let's look again at Nauny's papyrus.

88
00:06:28,287 --> 00:06:30,989
Anubis is reading the scale.

89
00:06:31,209 --> 00:06:33,762
But he is not alone in the scene.

90
00:06:33,982 --> 00:06:39,805
There is a god who records the result,
and a god who receives the dead.

91
00:06:40,025 --> 00:06:45,357
Once we separate these roles,
the idea of Anubis as a judge

92
00:06:45,357 --> 00:06:50,872
who decides everything on his own
turns out to be too simple.

93
00:06:51,800 --> 00:06:54,949
On this fragment of cartonnage, Thoth,

94
00:06:54,949 --> 00:06:59,009
with the head of an ibis,
holds writing equipment.

95
00:06:59,229 --> 00:07:05,870
The museum describes the scene as Thoth
introducing the deceased to Osiris.

96
00:07:06,090 --> 00:07:11,336
The clue to identifying a god
is not just the shape of the head.

97
00:07:11,556 --> 00:07:17,067
What the god holds, and what the god
is doing, matter just as much.

98
00:07:17,967 --> 00:07:21,224
According to the museum,
if the scale balanced,

99
00:07:21,224 --> 00:07:25,473
the deceased was accepted
by Osiris and granted eternal life.

100
00:07:25,693 --> 00:07:28,448
If not, the heart
was devoured by a creature

101
00:07:28,448 --> 00:07:31,139
called Ammut, and the person died forever.

102
00:07:31,359 --> 00:07:35,067
Anubis weighs, Thoth
records, Osiris receives.

103
00:07:35,287 --> 00:07:39,017
Judgment was pictured
as a scene involving many gods.

104
00:07:42,033 --> 00:07:45,958
The judgment scene does
have its frightening side.

105
00:07:46,178 --> 00:07:51,316
But the sources prepared for the dead
were not about failure alone.

106
00:07:51,536 --> 00:07:57,603
They were about passing the test safely
and moving on to the next existence.

107
00:07:57,823 --> 00:08:01,877
Seen through his role
as a protector, the same scene

108
00:08:01,877 --> 00:08:05,535
of the scale begins
to look a little different.

109
00:08:07,367 --> 00:08:13,530
Now for a somewhat unexpected object:
a relief showing King Amenemhat I.

110
00:08:13,750 --> 00:08:17,471
On either side of the king
stand Horus and Anubis,

111
00:08:17,471 --> 00:08:20,508
offering him the ankh, the sign of life.

112
00:08:20,728 --> 00:08:23,959
The museum connects this
scene to royal ritual.

113
00:08:24,179 --> 00:08:28,402
Anubis appears in more places
than the weighing of the heart.

114
00:08:30,667 --> 00:08:33,646
Here the king demonstrates,
through ritual,

115
00:08:33,646 --> 00:08:37,831
that even after a long reign
he still has the power to rule.

116
00:08:38,051 --> 00:08:43,745
Anubis was connected to this place
in his role as lord of the necropolis.

117
00:08:43,965 --> 00:08:47,486
The relief dates to around 1980 BCE.

118
00:08:47,706 --> 00:08:51,305
Set beside later statues,
it shows how the same

119
00:08:51,305 --> 00:08:55,138
god was spoken of across
a very long span of time.

120
00:08:56,067 --> 00:08:59,352
Much later, Anubis also
appears on a fragment

121
00:08:59,352 --> 00:09:02,039
of cartonnage from the Roman period.

122
00:09:02,259 --> 00:09:06,919
Osiris sits at the center,
with Thoth and other gods nearby.

123
00:09:07,139 --> 00:09:10,366
Long after, the relationship
between the dead

124
00:09:10,366 --> 00:09:13,153
and the gods was still being depicted.

125
00:09:13,373 --> 00:09:18,673
Still, we need not treat each object
as a copy of exactly the same story.

126
00:09:19,833 --> 00:09:24,214
For this small gold figure,
the museum offers two readings:

127
00:09:24,214 --> 00:09:27,386
it may be Anubis, or it
may be another god.

128
00:09:27,606 --> 00:09:31,743
The museum states clearly
that its identity is uncertain.

129
00:09:31,963 --> 00:09:36,859
Leaving the unknown as unknown
is part of telling history honestly.

130
00:09:37,079 --> 00:09:40,726
A sharp image does not make
an explanation certain.

131
00:09:42,300 --> 00:09:47,615
Beyond large paintings and statues,
there is also a figure of Anubis

132
00:09:47,615 --> 00:09:52,612
only about 7.7 centimeters tall,
made of bronze or copper alloy.

133
00:09:52,832 --> 00:09:56,827
Exploring museum collections
shows that grand myths were also

134
00:09:56,827 --> 00:10:00,423
expressed in objects small
enough to hold in your hand.

135
00:10:00,643 --> 00:10:02,502
Who used it, where, and how?

136
00:10:02,722 --> 00:10:05,239
Small objects open up new questions.

137
00:10:07,067 --> 00:10:12,010
Anubis was shown watching over
tombs, took part in the rites

138
00:10:12,010 --> 00:10:17,371
that prepared the body, and appears
in the weighing of the heart.

139
00:10:17,591 --> 00:10:24,771
He is a god deeply bound to death, but his
role cannot be summed up by fear alone.

140
00:10:24,991 --> 00:10:29,365
Protecting, and helping
the dead pass their test:

141
00:10:29,365 --> 00:10:34,195
the sources let us read him
as a god involved in both.

142
00:10:35,100 --> 00:10:42,141
From the story of the heart preserved
on papyrus, we can take away a question.

143
00:10:42,361 --> 00:10:48,750
If you could carry something beyond death,
what part of you would say who you were?

144
00:10:48,970 --> 00:10:53,330
Behind the images of the gods,
we can see people's

145
00:10:53,330 --> 00:10:57,067
wish to support the dead on their journey.

146
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:02,454
This video is based on public museum
collections and their descriptions.

147
00:11:02,674 --> 00:11:05,191
The narration uses synthetic speech,

148
00:11:05,191 --> 00:11:08,546
and some images are
AI-generated reconstructions.

149
00:11:08,766 --> 00:11:13,850
Titles, sources, and terms of use for all
materials are listed in the description.

150
00:11:14,070 --> 00:11:15,615
Thank you for watching.
